{
  "id": "COMPOUND:16888 - PROTEIN:4045",
  "verbalization": "Theasaponin E1 appears to have a shared target of K-252a, which in turn targets Pol polyprotein through multiple pathways. The first pathway links Theasaponin E1 to the phosphatidylinositol 4-phosphate 3-kinase C2 domain-containing subunit beta, which is a known target of K-252a. The second pathway connects Theasaponin E1 to solute carrier organic anion transporter family member 2A1, another target of K-252a, and both of these are also targets of the same inhibitor. The third pathway provides a more complex target, a phospholipid derivative, which still ultimately points to Pol polyprotein as the final target. This suggests that Theasaponin E1 may act through a variety of mechanisms, possibly involving different molecular pathways, to inhibit Pol polyprotein. The recurring involvement of K-252a as a common target and the presence of multiple distinct targets indicate that Theasaponin E1 may exert its effects through diverse, yet interconnected, biological processes.",
